    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from Spanish “montesano” meaning “mountain of health,” chosen for its pleasant sound.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montesano%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1853 Isaiah and Lorinda Scammon filed a 640‑acre Donation Land Claim at the Chehalis‑Wynoochee confluence, laying the town’s foundation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montesano%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The first permanent business, a mercantile store, opened in 1872 by John Esmond, marking Montesano’s commercial start.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.historylink.org/File/23361"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Montesano’s historic Scammon home, later called Scammon’s Hotel, served as a key river stop for pioneers and early seagoing ships.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montesano%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
